# The Player's settings. Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored — never
# commit real values. The server's settings live in Arrt's own .env.example
# (https://github.com/brookstalley/arrt), on whatever host runs the server.
#
# Values marked REQUIRED have no default: the Player refuses to start without
# them, naming the variable. That is deliberate — a plausible-looking default for
# CACHE_DIR would cache every wall in the wrong place and look like it had worked.
#
# PRECEDENCE: this file supplies defaults, and anything already exported wins.
# The Player resolves this file from its own config module's directory upward and
# never from the working directory, so `cd`-ing somewhere else does not pick up a
# different .env — exporting the variable is the only lever, which is why it has
# to be the winning one.

# --- The Player ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A Player is a CLIENT of the server: one install, one token, showing any number
# of walls, each on one of its outputs. It is told three things below and learns
# its walls from the server, so assigning a wall to it — or taking one away, or
# moving it to another output — needs no edit here (clients.md). The settings a
# Player configured for one wall used — WALL_ID, WALL_TOKEN and MANIFEST_SOURCE —
# are retired, and a Player refuses to start while any of them is set, naming
# what replaced it.
#
# Its OUTPUTS, by the names it reports to the server and a curator assigns a
# wall to:
#   frame      the Samsung Frame at TV_ADDRESS below, over its network API. A
#              client has one when TV_ADDRESS is set, and none when it is not.
#   hdmi-a-1   an HDMI connector on this host, as the kernel lists it
#   hdmi-a-2   (/sys/class/drm/card*-HDMI-A-<n>), reported whether or not a
#              screen is plugged in. A wall on one is drawn fitted to the screen.

# REQUIRED by the Player. The server's address as this host reaches it, e.g.
# http://127.0.0.1:8770 when both run on one machine.
SERVER_URL=
# REQUIRED by the Player. This client's token, issued once on the server's
# Settings › Clients when the client is added. It is shown once; keep it here and
# nowhere else; it never appears in the journal.
CLIENT_TOKEN=
# REQUIRED by the Player. Where it keeps what it pulls: the last client document,
# and for each wall a directory named by the wall's id holding its manifest, its
# renders, its heartbeat and, on the Frame, the Frame's store. Local disk, not a
# network mount: the point of the cache is that it is there when the server is
# not — a Player restarted while the server is down shows every wall it last
# knew from here. An absolute path (a leading ~ is expanded); the Player refuses
# a relative one rather than resolve it against wherever it was started.
CACHE_DIR=

# Optional. PLAYER. The TELEVISION's panel — never the e-paper one. The player
# composes every work on the Frame with it, because the mat is specified in
# physical units and is wrong on a panel of a different size. The server does
# not read it. The defaults describe a REFERENCE 42" 4K
# Frame — not any particular deployment, and not the one this repository was
# developed against, which is 50". Nothing in source hardcodes a panel.
#
# CHECK THE DIAGONAL AGAINST YOUR ACTUAL SET. It is "optional" only in the sense
# that it has a default, and that default is a plausible wrong answer rather than
# an obvious one — unlike SERVER_URL or CACHE_DIR, which refuse to start when unset,
# a stale diagonal produces a running system that quietly mis-sizes every mat on
# the wall. This bit a real deployment on
# 2026-08-04: the value read 42 while the set was a 50" QN50LS03DAFXZA, and nothing
# anywhere reported a problem. The set names its own size: the /api/v2/ REST
# endpoint reports modelName, whose "QN50" prefix is the diagonal. That endpoint
# and no other — the art channel's own get_device_info() carries rotation and
# sensor state and no model at all, and this paragraph named it as a source for
# two days, which is how the check below came to be written against a field that
# was never there.
#
# THAT IS NOW CHECKED, and this paragraph advertised it for two days before it
# was. `tools/tv_api_check.py` compares this value against what the set reports
# and fails its "panel size" check on a disagreement, naming both the pixels per
# inch you are judging at and the ones you should be. It cannot run from the
# server, which has no client for the television; run it on the Player's host,
# with the set awake, whenever this value or the set changes.
#
# It stays quiet about model lines the parse has not been verified against — one
# real set, one verified string. A quiet result is reported as "not compared" and
# names which side said nothing, so it can never be read as "confirmed correct":
# only a line that quotes the model and this value has compared them.
TV_PANEL_WIDTH_PX=3840
TV_PANEL_HEIGHT_PX=2160
TV_PANEL_DIAGONAL_INCHES=42

# Optional. PLAYER. The mat, in inches on the wall — the sides and top take
# MAT_WIDTH_INCHES, and the bottom takes that times MAT_BOTTOM_WEIGHT, because a
# true-centred image reads as sitting low. Physical units so the same numbers
# mean the same thing on any panel size. The mat takes the work's shape and the
# rest of the screen is black.
MAT_WIDTH_INCHES=1.5
MAT_BOTTOM_WEIGHT=1.15

# Optional, and the switch for this Player's `frame` output: set, the Player has
# a Frame at this LAN address and the TV_*, EPD_*, brightness and LOCATION keys
# below apply to it; empty, it has none and none of them are read.
TV_ADDRESS=
# Optional. Samsung secure websocket port; the same on every installation.
TV_PORT=8002
# Optional. Where the TV pairing token is stored. Defaults to
# $CACHE_DIR/token_file. A Player paired before it was a client kept it at
# $ART_ROOT/token_file: point this there to keep that pairing rather than accept a
# new prompt at the set. Never place this inside the checkout — that is how the
# previous token came to be committed. When set, an absolute path, as CACHE_DIR.
TV_TOKEN_FILE=
# Optional. The name the Player pairs to the television under, default
# `tvpi`. **Changing it costs a pairing prompt somebody has to walk over and
# accept**: the set issues a token per client name, so a new name arrives as a
# new client and the token above will not be honoured. It matches what the 2024
# loader paired under, so the process replacing it continues as the same client.
TV_CLIENT_NAME=

# --- The Player's own dials -------------------------------------------------
#
# Every one of these has a default that is the reference deployment's value, and
# every one is here because nothing may hardcode a panel, a pace, or a timeout.

# Optional. The e-paper label panel in pixels, default 1448x1072 (IT8951).
# **This is the label panel, never the television.** The television's geometry
# is TV_PANEL_* above.
EPD_PANEL_WIDTH_PX=1448
EPD_PANEL_HEIGHT_PX=1072

# Optional, with or without TV_ADDRESS: the label panel is a label output of this
# client, named `epd-0`, which the server maps to any wall on any client (Walls).
# omni-epd's identifier for this device's e-paper panel — the reference
# wall's is `waveshare_epd.it8951`, and `omni_epd.mock` drives the library's own
# no-op device on a machine with the driver installed and no panel attached.
#
# **Leave it empty and this device draws no label, which is a supported
# deployment rather than a broken one.** Most display devices are a television and
# nothing else; the label is an annotation of the wall, never a precondition for
# it. A panel named here that will not open costs the label and nothing else — the
# walls go on rotating, the journal says so, and the client heartbeat reports the
# label output `connected: false`, which is a different thing from a client that
# reports none.
#
# The two optional dependency groups this needs are NOT installed by a default
# `uv sync`: `uv sync --group raster --group epaper`.
EPD_DEVICE=

# **REQUIRED if EPD_DEVICE is set, and the only two values here with no default.**
# The label panel's diagonal, and the distance somebody reads it from. Inches for
# both, so nobody has to remember which one is feet — the reference wall is a
# 6-inch panel read from 7 feet.
#
# Together they decide how large the label's type has to be, which is not a thing
# that can be guessed: a wrong distance produces type that is silently illegible,
# and it looks like success from every direction except standing in front of the
# panel. That is not hypothetical. This product shipped a 26 px body size — a cap
# height of 2.5 arcminutes at 7 feet, against the 5 that 20/20 vision needs to
# resolve a letter at all — through a hardware probe, a review and a cutover,
# because nothing anywhere converted pixels into the angle a person actually sees.
#
# So there is no fallback. A device with a panel configured and these unstated
# draws no label and says which key is missing, while the television goes on
# rotating: nothing about the label may stop the wall.
#
# **Left empty here on purpose, like SERVER_URL and TV_ADDRESS above.** A `.env` is
# copied from this file once and lived in, so a value written here is inherited by
# every deployment that copies it — and a second wall that never measured would
# then run the *reference* wall's 7 feet with no error, no missing-key message and
# a startup line that prints a copied number exactly like a measured one. That is
# the guessed distance this whole pair exists to prevent, arriving by the back
# door. Empty, the same installer loses only the label and is told which key to
# set.
#
# The reference wall's values are 6 and 84 — a 6-inch panel read from 7 feet.
# Measure to where people actually stand, not to where you stand while installing
# it.
EPD_PANEL_DIAGONAL_INCHES=
EPD_VIEWING_DISTANCE_INCHES=

# Optional, and normally left alone: the clear border around the label in pixels.
# **It derives from the type by default**, because a border trades directly
# against how many lines survive the label's drop rule and so cannot be picked
# apart from the floor that decides how many lines there are — and that floor now
# comes from the two values above.
#
# Set it only for a surface whose border is a physical fact rather than a
# typographic choice: a device drawing its label into the mat area around an
# artwork does not get to choose where the picture ends.
#
# **Left commented deliberately.** A `.env` is copied from this file once and
# lived in, so a value written here would quietly override the derivation forever
# on every deployment that copied it — which is the failure this whole pair of
# settings exists to prevent, arriving by the back door.
#EPD_MARGIN_PX=

# Optional. How far the rendered label is turned before it reaches the panel,
# default 180 — which is what the reference wall runs, its panel being mounted
# with the ribbon uppermost. Only 0 and 180 are accepted: a quarter turn exchanges
# the panel's width and height, so the label would have to be laid out against the
# swapped geometry to come out right.
EPD_ROTATE_DEGREES=180

# Optional. How often each wall's worker reads its cached manifest, and its pull
# asks the server for a new one, default 1.0 seconds. **The interval is set by `next`, not by theme switching**: a theme
# change tolerates seconds of latency happily, while a human pressing "next" and
# waiting three seconds thinks the product is broken. One stat() a second is free.
MANIFEST_POLL_SECONDS=1.0

# Optional. The television's brightness scale, which is neither 0-100 nor 0-10:
# this generation takes -4 through 10, and the sun-position curve is mapped onto
# whatever range is given here. Carried forward from the 2024 plane.
TV_MIN_BRIGHTNESS=-4
TV_MAX_BRIGHTNESS=10
# Optional. How often the sun is re-consulted, default 300 seconds. The
# television is written to only when the computed value *changes*, so this costs
# a local calculation and no traffic.
BRIGHTNESS_INTERVAL_SECONDS=300

# Optional. The wait for the set's acknowledgement of an upload, default 60
# seconds. **A correctness value, not a tuning knob.** The library's own default
# of 10 was measured failing on this deployment's 4K composites *while the image
# landed anyway* — so a caller at the default is told an upload failed that
# succeeded, and a retry duplicates it on the wall.
TV_UPLOAD_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60
# Optional. Ceiling on opening the art channel, default 30 seconds.
# `start_listening()` has been observed **hanging** rather than raising, which for
# an unattended daemon is a wedge, not an error. Not caused by art mode being off:
# the channel opens against a dark panel, and the hang has been seen in art mode
# after many connections in quick succession.
TV_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30
# Optional. How long a work whose upload failed is left alone before it is tried
# again, default 300 seconds. A television that is reachable and refuses one
# image is a different fault from one that is asleep, and it does not back off
# with the connection — without this the pass retries every second, each attempt
# costing a round trip and a row rewritten onto an SD card.
TV_UPLOAD_RETRY_SECONDS=300
# Optional. How long the set is given to actually show an image after it is
# selected, default 8 seconds. **A selection is confirmed rather than assumed.**
# A television whose panel is dark accepts the request, returns no error, emits
# no event and goes on displaying what it had — so a daemon that trusted the call
# would report a rotation it never performed, once per interval, all night. A
# real set agrees in about two seconds; this is sized well above that.
TV_SELECT_CONFIRM_SECONDS=8
# Optional. Reconnection backoff bounds in seconds, default 5 and 300. An asleep
# set is the expected overnight condition rather than an incident.
TV_RETRY_MIN_SECONDS=5
TV_RETRY_MAX_SECONDS=300

# REQUIRED with TV_ADDRESS. Drives the Frame's sun-position brightness. Decimal
# degrees; south and west are negative.
LATITUDE=
LONGITUDE=
# REQUIRED with TV_ADDRESS. Human-readable place name, used for timezone-aware
# sun calculation.
LOCATION_NAME=
# Optional. Region/country, cosmetic.
LOCATION_REGION=
